A root component's own state, on every target.

`component main root { … }` renders windows rather than widgets, and
passRootWindow lifts those windows out of it -- which used to leave the vars and
funcs it declared on a shell nothing renders. `var hits = 0` reached no backend
at all: html emitted `var state = {}`, and the read of it rendered against a
binding the page never declared (#215).

A *timer* is not hoisted and is not covered here. It is an ordinary statement in
the body rather than a declaration on the component, and the body is cleared --
so a `time.timer(…)` written in a root component, or at the root of a file, is
dropped in silence. That is pre-existing on both paths and outside #215; see
applyRootWindow's note.

They are hoisted into the window the component lifts, because that is where
they are mounted. A window is a state owner every consumer already reads, and
its funcs are already Model methods, so what each target emits below is the
shape it emits for a window's own state -- a Model field on bubbletea and fyne,
a `remember { mutableStateOf }` on android, an entry in html's `state` object.

A component-body func is a method and a window-body one is not, so the hoist
drops the receiver with them: `bump` is emitted under its own name, not as a
method of the shell it was written on.

deny * `(?i)\bmain_?bump\b` -- the hoisted func is the window's, so no target spells it as a method of the component it was written on
deny out/none/html/index.html `var state = \{\s*\}` -- the state is the window's now; an empty state object is the bug this fixture is for

-- app.sngl --
import ui "sngl:ui"

output {
    go {
        bubbletea
        fyne
    }
    none {
        html
    }
    kotlin {
        android
    }
}

component main ui.root {
    var hits = 0

    func bump(n int) => n + 1

    ui.window #page(title="t") {
        ui.text(value="{hits}")
        ui.button(text="go", @click { hits = bump(hits) })
    }
}
